A||||6||Predator||White smoke from exhaust||||5||
Z||000000||Predator||12-19-2000||07:39 AM||||I have a 1985 Fiero GT. Does anybody know the symptom of white smoke coming from the exhaust could be. The car runs fine, but it is putting out a lot of white smoke. I am not driving it until I can figure out what happened. Thanks. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||12-19-2000||08:17 AM||||White smoke is an almost sure sign of coolant in the cylinders(cracked head, blown head gasket etc.). Do a compression check, most likely you'll find that 2 adjacent cyl. will be lower than the rest.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Predator||12-19-2000||08:29 AM||||Thanks, I was afraid you were going to say that. Well I guess the old girl is going to be garaged for a while. I cannot afford to have that kind of work done right now. ||||reg||9
Z||000003||msweldon||12-19-2000||01:13 PM||||was this the same Gt you put the turbo on?<P>I hope not...||||reg||1
Z||000004||DavidHenshaw||12-19-2000||03:01 PM||||White smoke before the engine has warmed up is normal. After the engine is warmed its a problem. <P>A blown head gasket or cracked head or engine block will usually cause the oil to look milky or coffee colored. Coolant may also seep out where the exhaust manifold meets the exhaust pipe. Another give away is oil getting into the coolant system. Look at the coolant overflow bottle up front for abnormal looking coolant.<P>If you have an automatic transmission white smoke can occur without there being a cracked head or blown gasket. If the transmission uses a vacuum modulator and the diaphragm is cracked the vacuum created by the engine will suck transmission fluid into the combustion chambers. Check transmission fluid level, if low you may just be burning transmission fluid. If this seems to be the case remove the vacuum line from the vacuum modulator and run a pipe cleaner into the modulator. If it has oil on it when pulled back out replace the modulator.<P>If it turns out to be a blown head gasket thats pretty easy to fix. Cracked heads or blocks are different stories. <P>Lastly, if coolant has gotten inside the engine it can do extensive damage to internal engine parts requiring a complete engine overhaul as opposed to a simple head gasket replacement. Moral of this story is to pay close attention to condition of oil at all times.<P>Dave<P>86 GT ||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||12-19-2000||05:04 PM||||Normally you can smell the coolant in the exhaust when something is wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000006||LAMBO||12-20-2000||01:13 PM||||This is going to sound a little silly, but...<BR>hasn't it gotten a little cold down in GA lately. Up here in Iowa, when temps fall below 30 deg., all our cars make "white" smoke (moist, hot exhaust mixing with cold, sub-freezing air). Maybe you're just not used to seeing this.<P>I know, I'll go crawl back under my rock now.<BR>TL||||reg||12
